:: StoryTitle
Twee Adventure Tutorial

:: Start
# Welcome to Twee Adventures

This is a tutorial for writing **Twee-based CYOA adventures** with this framework.

The framework loads exactly one external story file:

`story.twee`

Your HTML file and `story.twee` must be in the same directory. For example:

`index.html`

`story.twee`

The file name must be exactly:

`story.twee`

These files will not be loaded automatically:

- `adventure.twee`
- `my-story.twee`
- `chapter1.twee`
- `story.txt`

If `story.twee` cannot be loaded, the framework uses its embedded fallback story instead.

Choose a topic:

* [[How Twee files work->Twee Basics]]
* [[Creating choices and links->Choices]]
* [[Using Markdown formatting->Markdown]]
* [[Planning an adventure->Adventure Design]]
* [[Understanding the required file name->File Name]]
* [[Read the summary->Summary]]

:: File Name
# The Required File Name

This framework looks for one specific file:

`story.twee`

It does not search the directory for Twee files, and it does not automatically load files with other names.

Your project should look like this:

`index.html`

`story.twee`

The HTML page runs:

`fetch("story.twee")`

That means the browser requests a file named `story.twee` from the same directory as the HTML page.

For example, this structure works:

`my-adventure/index.html`

`my-adventure/story.twee`

This structure does not work automatically:

`my-adventure/index.html`

`my-adventure/adventure.twee`

To use a different filename, you would need to change the JavaScript request in the HTML file. For example:

`fetch("adventure.twee")`

However, without changing the JavaScript, only `story.twee` will be loaded.

When `story.twee` is missing or cannot be loaded, the embedded fallback story is displayed instead.

[[Return to the tutorial->Start]]

:: Twee Basics
# How Twee Files Work

A Twee file is made up of passages. Each passage begins with a header:

`:: Passage Name`

Everything after that header belongs to the passage until the next `::` header.

For example:

`:: Forest`

`You enter a dark forest.`

Passage names are converted into links by changing them to lowercase and replacing spaces with hyphens.

Therefore, this passage:

`:: Dark Forest`

has the internal link name:

`dark-forest`

The first ordinary passage in the file becomes the starting passage unless a `StoryData` passage specifies a different start passage.

The `StoryTitle` passage is special. Its content becomes the document title, so it is not displayed as an ordinary passage.

[[Learn about choices->Choices]]

[[Back to the tutorial->Start]]

:: Choices
# Creating Choices and Links

Choices connect one passage to another.

The simplest link uses the passage name as both its label and destination:

`[[Forest]]`

A link can also use a different label:

`[[Enter the forest->Forest]]`

The destination appears after the arrow. Passage names are not case-sensitive for links, and spaces are converted to hyphens.

You can also write links in reverse form:

`[[Forest<-Enter the forest]]`

Or use a vertical bar:

`[[Enter the forest|Forest]]`

For example:

* [[Open the wooden door->Wooden Door]]
* [[Climb the stairs->Stairs]]
* [[Return to the choices lesson->Choices]]

A link to a passage that does not exist is displayed in red and produces an error if selected.

